#: ../../../userguide/pyproject_config.rst:236
msgid ""
"Supporting ``file`` for dependencies is meant for a convenience for "
"packaging applications with possibly strictly versioned dependencies."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/pyproject_config.rst:239
msgid ""
"Library packagers are discouraged from using overly strict (or \"locked\") "
"dependency versions in their ``dependencies`` and ``optional-dependencies``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/pyproject_config.rst:242
msgid ""
"Currently, when specifying ``optional-dependencies`` dynamically, all of the "
"groups must be specified dynamically; one can not specify some of them "
"statically and some of them dynamically."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/pyproject_config.rst:246
msgid ""
"Also note that the file format for specifying dependencies resembles a "
"``requirements.txt`` file, however please keep in mind that all non-comment "
"lines must conform with :pep:`508` (``pip`` specific syntaxes, e.g. ``-c/-r/-"
"e`` and other flags, are not supported)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/pyproject_config.rst:252
msgid ""
"If you are using an old version of ``setuptools``, you might need to ensure "
"that all files referenced by the ``file`` directive are included in the "
"``sdist`` (you can do that via ``MANIFEST.in`` or using plugins such as "
"``setuptools-scm``, please have a look on :doc:`/userguide/miscellaneous` "
"for more information)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/pyproject_config.rst:257
msgid ""
"Newer versions of ``setuptools`` will automatically add these files to the "
"``sdist``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/pyproject_config.rst:260
msgid ""
"It is advisable to use literal values together with ``attr`` (e.g. ``str``, "
"``tuple[str]``, see :func:`ast.literal_eval`). This is recommend in order to "
"support the common case of a literal value assigned to a variable in a "
"module containing (directly or indirectly) third-party imports."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/pyproject_config.rst:265
msgid ""
"``attr`` first tries to read the value from the module by examining the "
"module's AST. If that fails, ``attr`` falls back to importing the module, "
"using :func:`importlib.util.spec_from_file_location` recommended recipe "
"(see :ref:`example on Python docs <python:importlib-examples>` about "
"\"Importing a source file directly\"). Note however that importing the "
"module is error prone since your package is not installed yet. You may also "
"need to manually add the project directory to ``sys.path`` (via ``setup."
"py``) in order to be able to do that."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/pyproject_config.rst:278
msgid ""
"``pip`` may allow editable install only with ``pyproject.toml`` and ``setup."
"cfg``. However, this behavior may not be consistent over various ``pip`` "
"versions and other packaging-related tools (``setup.py`` is more reliable on "
"those scenarios)."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/pyproject_config.rst:283
msgid ""
"Dynamic ``scripts`` and ``gui-scripts`` are a special case. When resolving "
"these metadata keys, ``setuptools`` will look for ``tool.setuptools.dynamic."
"entry-points``, and use the values of the ``console_scripts`` and "
"``gui_scripts`` :doc:`entry-point groups <PyPUG:specifications/entry-"
"points>`."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/pyproject_config.rst:289
msgid ""
"In the context of this document, *directives* are special TOML values that "
"are interpreted differently by ``setuptools`` (usually triggering an "
"associated function). Most of the times they correspond to a special TOML "
"table (or inline-table) with a single top-level key. For example, you can "
"have the ``{find = {where = [\"src\"], exclude=[\"tests*\"]}}`` directive "
"for ``tool.setuptools.packages``, or ``{attr = \"mymodule.attr\"}`` "
"directive for ``tool.setuptools.dynamic.version``."
msgstr ""

#: ../../../userguide/pyproject_config.rst:297
msgid ""
"``attr`` is meant to be used when the module attribute is statically "
"specified (e.g. as a string). As a rule of thumb, the attribute should be "
"able to be parsed with :func:`ast.literal_eval`, and should not be modified "
"or re-assigned."
msgstr ""
